    {
      "heading": "Mumbai's Best-Kept Secret",
      "text": "Gorai is the Mumbai that the rest of Mumbai doesn't know about. While Juhu and Versova get all the attention, Gorai's beach - longer, wider and significantly less crowded - sits quietly at the northwestern tip of the city, accessible by a 10-minute ferry ride across the mangrove-lined creek from Borivali Jetty. The ferry itself is part of the experience: the transition from the noise and density of Borivali to the quiet, green, village-paced world of Gorai takes exactly ten minutes and feels like a journey of a thousand kilometres. It is one of Mumbai's most extraordinary daily commutes, and the residents who make it every day know they have something the rest of the city can only dream of."
    },
    {
      "heading": "The East Indian Community - Mumbai's Original Inhabitants",
      "text": "Gorai's soul is the East Indian Catholic community - one of Mumbai's oldest and most distinctive indigenous groups, descended from the original inhabitants of the Konkan coast who converted to Christianity under the Portuguese in the 16th century. They have lived in Gorai for centuries, fishing the creek, farming the land and celebrating their feasts with a fervour that has not diminished with urbanisation. The St. Bonaventure Church feast in July is the community's most important annual celebration - a novena, a grand feast day Mass, a village fair and the traditional East Indian feast food that is prepared only for this occasion. The East Indian bottle masala - a complex spice blend unique to this community - is one of India's most extraordinary and least-known culinary traditions, and Gorai is one of the few places in Mumbai where you can still taste it in its authentic form."
    },
    {
      "heading": "The Global Vipassana Pagoda - A Monument to Peace",
      "text": "The Global Vipassana Pagoda is one of the most extraordinary buildings in India - a massive stone dome built without any supporting pillars, housing a meditation hall that can seat 8,000 people simultaneously. Built as a monument to S.N. Goenka and the Vipassana meditation tradition, it rises magnificently from the banks of Gorai Creek, visible from the ferry and from the beach. The Pagoda hosts 10-day Vipassana meditation courses and day-long meditation sittings throughout the year, open to all and free of charge. It draws practitioners from across India and the world to Gorai's peaceful creek-side setting, adding an international, spiritual dimension to a neighbourhood that is otherwise deeply local and community-rooted."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Living in Gorai - The Trade-Off Worth Making",
      "text": "Living in Gorai requires accepting a trade-off: the ferry stops at around 10pm, the road via Gorai Link Road is slow, the shopping options are limited and the major hospitals are in Borivali. In exchange, you get a beach at your doorstep, clean air, a genuine community, affordable rents, the Global Vipassana Pagoda as your neighbour and a quality of life that is simply not available anywhere else in Mumbai at this price point. The young families and working professionals who have discovered Gorai in recent years have made this trade-off consciously and enthusiastically. Mumbai96 is proud to serve Gorai's community - from the East Indian family that needs a reliable plumber for their village house to the young professional who wants the best salon on Gorai Link Road."
    }
  ],
